    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Radius of convergence dictates by complex singularities [S+N]\n\nMaxima can compute the Taylor expansion of $1/(1 + z^2)$ at $z = 0$. The coefficients alternate as $0, 0, -1, 0, 1, 0, -1, \\ldots$, giving radius $R = 1$ — the distance to the nearest singularity, which is $\\pm i$ (where $1 + z^2 = 0$).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "series_atan : taylor(1/(1 + z^2), z, 0, 12)$\nprint(\"Taylor series of 1/(1 + z²):\"); series_atan;\nprint()$\nprint(\"Singularities (zeros of 1 + z²):\")$\nsolve(1 + z^2 = 0, z);",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Taylor series of 1/(1 + z²):\n\"Taylor series of 1/(1 + z²):\"\n1-z^2+z^4-z^6+z^8-z^10+z^12\nSingularities (zeros of 1 + z²):"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ z=-i , z=i \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 14,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The singularities at $\\pm i$ are at distance 1 from the origin. The series converges on $|z| < 1$ and diverges on $|z| > 1$ — including for *real* $|x| > 1$, even though $1/(1 + x^2)$ is perfectly smooth on all of $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nIn real analysis we couldn't see *why* the radius was 1. Now we can: **the radius of convergence equals the distance from the centre to the nearest singularity in $\\mathbb{C}$.**\n\n## Holomorphic ⇒ analytic on disks\n\nA theorem we won't prove here but is the heart of complex analysis: any function holomorphic on an open set is **analytic** — locally equal to its convergent Taylor series. The proof goes through Cauchy's integral formula (notebook 5).\n\nConsequences:\n\n- **$C^\\infty$**: holomorphic functions are infinitely differentiable. (One complex derivative ⇒ all of them.)\n- **Identity theorem**: two holomorphic functions agreeing on a set with a limit point (e.g. an interval, a sequence with limit in the domain) agree on the entire connected component.\n- **Liouville**: a bounded entire function (holomorphic on all of $\\mathbb{C}$) is constant.\n\nNone of these holds for real $C^\\infty$ functions.\n\n## Why $1/(1+x^2)$ doesn't have a real-analogue explanation\n\nReal $C^\\infty$ allows the bump function $e^{-1/x^2}$ — equal to its Taylor series only at the single point of expansion. Complex $C^\\infty$ (i.e., holomorphic) doesn't allow this — *the existence of one complex derivative drags everything else along*.\n\nThe reason is integration: $f'(z)$ existing on a disk lets us write $f$ as an integral of itself around small circles (the Cauchy integral formula), and that integral is itself analytic. So holomorphy chains: derivatives exist, integrals over closed paths are zero, the function is analytic. We'll see this machinery in the next notebooks.\n\n## Numerical demonstration: rate of convergence inside R [S+N]\n\nTake $f(z) = e^z$ (entire, $R = \\infty$). Truncating at order $N$ and evaluating at $z = 1 + i$, the error decays factorially in $N$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "z_eval : 1.0 + 1.0 * %i$\ntruth : float(exp(1.0)) * (cos(1.0) + %i * sin(1.0))$\nprint(\"e^{1+i} (truth) =\", truth)$\n\nerrs : []$\nfor N in [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 14, 18] do block([s, n, err],\n  s : 0,\n  for n : 0 thru N do s : s + z_eval^n / float(n!),\n  err : float(cabs(s - truth)),\n  print(\"N =\", N, \"  |error| =\", err),\n  errs : endcons(err, errs))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 15,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "e^{1+i} (truth) = 2.718281828459045*(0.8414709848078965*%i+0.5403023058681398)\nN = 2   |error| = 0.6040357527098376\nN = 4   |error| = 0.055624186521464655\nN = 6   |error| = 0.0025420013831730726\nN = 8   |error| = 6.888597105761968e-5\nN = 10   |error| = 1.2319456177166703e-6\nN = 14   |error| = 1.4733869315173022e-10\nN = 18   |error| = 6.141450319825084e-15"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## When boundary behaviour matters\n\nOn the boundary of the disk of convergence, anything can happen:\n\n- $\\sum z^n$ at $z = 1$: terms don't go to 0; diverges.\n- $\\sum z^n / n$ at $z = 1$: harmonic series; diverges.\n- $\\sum z^n / n$ at $z = -1$: alternating harmonic; converges to $-\\ln 2$.\n- $\\sum z^n / n^2$: converges absolutely on the closed unit disk by Weierstrass M-test.\n\nBoundary behaviour ties into Fourier theory and analytic continuation — beautiful but heavy machinery for another time.\n\n## What's next\n\nWe can now talk about Taylor series in $\\mathbb{C}$ confidently. The next two notebooks build the integration machinery — contour integrals, Cauchy's theorem, and Cauchy's integral formula — that lets us *prove* the analyticity claim and unlocks the residue calculus.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}